---
// Section 1 of the WE THE PEOPLE slide deck (3rd June 2026, To Share) — text only,
// one 70ch column. Design comes later; the words are the deck's, near-verbatim.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
---

<Layout title="The Idea — We The People" introLines={['The Idea']}>
  <PageHero lines={['The Idea']}>
    <p class="hero__kicker fade-up">Section 01 of the strategy</p>
  </PageHero>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__opener">We all know things are going wrong. We all feel it.</h2>

    <p class="lead">
      Everyone has their own list: cost of living, inequality, immigration, child poverty,
      global conflict — or maybe it's the potholes.
    </p>
    <p>
      So many of us feel angry, afraid, disillusioned — and above all, powerless.
    </p>

    <h2>System Failure</h2>
    <p>
      The people supposedly in charge — our politicians — are a big part of the problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      The criticisms are familiar: they're out for themselves. They're disconnected from
      us. They only care about media and money. The party system rewards loyalty, not
      excellence.
    </p>
    <p>No wonder trust in politicians is the lowest it's ever been.</p>
    <p>
      Even if there were a quality politician who wanted to do good, they'd still face
      the inertia and opacity of the State, fumbling in the dark for the levers of power.
    </p>
    <p>Things will not get better with just a different person at the wheel.</p>
    <p>We need systems change. We need something genuinely new.</p>

    <p class="pull">But if not to politicians, then where shall we turn?</p>

    <h2>The answer is us. The people ourselves.</h2>
    <p>
      For what we and the politicians have forgotten is this: the power was ours all
      along.
    </p>
    <p>
      Governments exist to serve the interests of their people. That is the source of
      their authority — and more importantly, their legitimacy.
    </p>
    <p>
      If our politicians no longer do that, it is our right to reclaim our power and
      create something that works for us again.
    </p>
    <p>But something stands in our way: how divided we are.</p>

    <h2>Healing Divisions</h2>
    <p>
      Stoked by the media, economic pressure and social alienation, our society feels
      more polarised than ever.<a href="#fn1" id="fn1-ref">*</a> Politicians increasingly
      pitch to those extremes. The centre cannot hold.
    </p>
    <p>But We The People still believe in democracy.</p>
    <p>
      That we can find a way to talk, compromise and forge a shared vision for our
      future, no matter how difficult that might seem right now.
    </p>
    <p>Thankfully a powerful democratic mechanism exists to do just that.</p>

    <h2>It's called a Citizens' Assembly</h2>
    <p>In simple terms it applies the principles of jury service to politics.</p>
    <p>
      A group of 100 or so ordinary people are selected at random to reflect the
      population as a whole by factors like class, age, gender, race etc.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are given a difficult societal problem to face. They listen to experts and
      witnesses from all sides of the debate and then talk among themselves to forge
      policy approaches that everyone can accept.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it works</h2>
    <p>
      Everyday people are at the heart of the process so the public feel better
      represented than by an ego-driven political class.
    </p>
    <p>
      Free from the pressures to win votes or funding, an assembly's decisions have
      greater legitimacy too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most importantly, impartial facilitation allows every perspective to be heard. That
      creates room for understanding, respect — even empathy.
    </p>

    <h2>We start to believe in “us” again</h2>
    <p>Assemblies are now springing up all over the world to tackle a range of problems.</p>
    <p>
      But we believe they work best when tackling the most divisive issues we face, in a
      way no other political approach can match.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Ireland, one helped people navigate the question of abortion. In France, on
      assisted dying. We even had one in the UK on climate change that led to rare
      agreement between left and right.
    </p>
    <p>
      This democratic mechanism offers hope for a peaceful, united path through our hour
      of crisis.
    </p>
    <p>This is how we can turn things around.</p>

    <p class="footnote" id="fn1">
      * <a
        href="https://www.ipsos.com/en-uk/uks-sense-division-reaches-new-high-culture-war-tensions-grow-study-finds"
        rel="noopener">Ipsos: UK's sense of division reaches new high as culture war
        tensions grow</a
      >
    </p>

    <a class="back" href="/">← Back</a>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    var back = document.querySelector('article.prose .back');
    if (!back) return;
    var fired = false;
    var observer = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      entries.forEach(function (entry) {
        if (entry.isIntersecting && !fired) {
          fired = true;
          window.posthog?.capture('idea_article_read');
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      });
    }, { threshold: 0.5 });
    observer.observe(back);
  });
</script>
